  "query_preview": "..\n(Saturday through Wednesday)\nIssued at 124 PM CST Thu Nov 12 2026\n\nPrecipitation shifts further east through Saturday morning and mid \nafternoon, settling over the Permian Basin as the shortwave ejects \nout over the southern Great Plains. There's some noticeable \ndiscrepancy between the deterministic models on where exactly (and \nhow much) rain falls on Saturday. The GFS is a bit on the drier \nside, which is likely the case of later formation of a surface low \nacross eastern NM, which itself is the result of a shallower trough \nmoving across CO. The ECMWF is wetter, with the surface low forming \nquicker as the trough across CO is deeper. Regardless of the \nsolution that plays out, rain continues to be favored across the \nPermian Basin eastward. As that aforementioned surface low develops, \nwinds look to pick up across our western areas and primarily near \nthe Guadalupe Mountains. With an increasing surface pressure \ngradient, west to southwest winds may gust up to 25-35mph, with \nlocalized gusts upward of 45mph near the Guadalupe Mountains.\n \nAfter the surface low progresses eastward through Saturday, it looks \npoised to send a cold front through our area Sunday night into \nMonday morning. Winds behind the front are not likely to be quite \nstrong at this time, but high temperatures will plummet on Monday \ncompared to previous days (currently forecasting low to mid 50s for \nhigh temperatures outside of areas right along the Rio Grande \nRiver). Not expecting precipitation with this front, though cannot \ncompletely rule out light drizzle and/or low clouds persisting at \ntimes. The next feature to watch will be another trough progressing \ninto western CONUS on Tuesday. Current guidance has some showers \nskirting through our New Mexico areas, though a deeper/stronger \ntrough may push showers further south into TX. The rest of the week \nlooks to be active with additional troughs approaching from the \nwest, along with the northward advection of Gulf moisture, but where \nexactly these features are able to set up will dictate the weather \nacross our area. Thus, there is low confidence in the forecast past \nTuesday at this time.",
